course: dictionary
lesson: 15648
title: The old word thence
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Thence» is an old word. You will meet it in old stories, old maps and formal writing. Most people today would say «from there» or «after that» instead.
explain: Look at the words around it: «They sailed to Dover, and thence to France.» Thence points back to a place the reader already knows. It means from there, so the travellers went on from Dover to France.
explain: Thence can also mean from that time, or after that. «The king was crowned in spring, and thence he ruled for forty years.» It can mean for that reason, too: «The bridge was closed, and thence we took the long way round.» Read the clues to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Thence is an old, formal word, so it is found mostly in old books and formal writing.
fact: «Thence» means from that place, as in «They went to London, and thence to Dover.»
fact: «Thence» can also mean from that time, or after that.
fact: «Thence» can mean for that reason, or therefore.
fact: To check your meaning, swap in «from there», «after that» or «therefore» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The old map showed the river, and thence the road to the hills.» What does thence mean here? | A kind of bird | Very quickly | *From that place, from there | Under the water
exercise: «The bridge was closed, and thence we took the long way round.» What does thence mean here? | Before that | At the top of the hill | Very happily | *Therefore, for that reason
exercise: «The king was crowned in spring, and thence he ruled for forty years.» Which meaning fits thence here? | Not at all | From that place | *From that time, after that | Under the table
test: «The path led down to the river, and thence across the meadow.» What does thence mean? | Quickly and quietly | Because of the rain | *From that place, from there | Before the sun rose
test: «The storm was fierce, and thence the ship stayed in harbour.» What does thence mean? | Loudly and proudly | *Therefore, for that reason | From that time, after that | Very far away
exam: «The letter arrived in May, and thence the family moved to the coast.» What does thence mean here? | *From that time, after that | Very quietly | Without any reason | From that place
